### Visual identity

I recommend a controlled combination of:

| Element              | Direction                                              |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| Primary background   | Deep navy / near-black                                 |
| Secondary background | Warm off-white                                         |
| Primary accent       | Restrained electric blue                               |
| Secondary accent     | Muted steel blue                                       |
| Typography           | Modern grotesk sans-serif                              |
| Technical typography | IBM Plex Mono-style                                    |
| Cards                | Subtle borders and layered surfaces                    |
| Graphics             | Original engineering diagrams                          |
| Motion               | Restrained, purposeful                                 |
| Photography          | Minimal; product and engineering visuals take priority |

Avoid making every page dark. A deliberate alternation between dark editorial sections and light information-heavy sections will provide visual rhythm.

### Typography

Use a two-family system:

- Display and body: Archivo or a similarly distinctive contemporary sans-serif.
- Technical labels: IBM Plex Mono.

Typography hierarchy:

- Oversized hero headline.
- Medium section headlines.
- Compact uppercase technical labels.
- Highly readable body copy.
- Monospaced metadata and step identifiers.

Do not use large typography everywhere. Large type should communicate hierarchy, not decoration.

### The full-width rule

This is particularly important given your previous feedback.

Wrong: narrow centered website

Excessive unused horizontal space; every section is trapped in one narrow column.

Correct: expansive, controlled composition

Full-width sections, expansive grids, and content-specific maximum widths.

Recommended layout constraints:

- Main page sections: approximately 100% available width.
- Desktop horizontal gutters: responsive, approximately 48–80px.
- Mobile horizontal gutters: approximately 20–24px.
- Hero composition: expansive, potentially split into two columns.
- Service grids: three columns on suitable desktop widths, two on tablets, one on narrow mobile.
- Content-heavy paragraphs: controlled reading width.
- Technical diagrams: dedicated full-width containers.

Never apply one global `max-width` to the entire page wrapper. That is how websites become visually compressed.

